          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows her present loving state. The following statement that he had not known it because of her fault frames the expression as a disclosure of previously concealed affection.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression does not create, renew, or invoke an obligation binding the rose. In the same turn she releases the little prince rather than promising continued care or requesting reciprocal commitment. The later account of responsibility concerns the little prince's bond to his rose and does not give this earlier avowal undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"Tu es responsable de ta rose...\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"This is a deliberate, explanatory disclosure rather than a reflex-like affective outburst. The surrounding scene explicitly characterizes the rose's manner as calm, despite the emotion she conceals.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core function is adequately captured as an avowal of love. Its contextual roles of confession and correction explain the avowal but do not constitute a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her earlier conduct, the rose calmly reveals that she loves him and accepts responsibility for having concealed it. She urges him to be happy and to leave rather than trying to keep her protected under the globe.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by the rose is established in this occurrence. Her avowal acknowledges affection but is accompanied by permission or encouragement to depart, not by a promise of future conduct. The little prince responds with concerns about wind and animals, showing care, but the supplied scene records no explicit acceptance of a commitment proposed by the rose. Later statements about responsibility belong to the little prince's understanding of their bond and are not uptake of a promise in the focal utterance.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince. The classification relies only on the supplied blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports genuine affection through the rose's concealed tears and the surrounding descriptions of tenderness, but it cannot conclusively establish inner sincerity beyond the represented narration. No omitted context is treated as absent from the work.\"}"
        }
      ],
